## 1. Make every rate card clickable

  Update renderForex() and renderCrypto() in js/main.js to render links:

  return `
    <a class="rate-card"
       href="/currency.html?type=forex&symbol=${sym}">
      <span class="rate-symbol">${sym}</span>
      <span class="rate-name">${FOREX_NAMES[sym]}</span>
      <span class="rate-value">${value.toFixed(4)}</span>
    </a>`;

  For cryptocurrency cards:

  href="/currency.html?type=crypto&symbol=${meta.symbol}"

  Then update the card CSS so links do not look like ordinary text links:

  .rate-card {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .rate-card:hover {
    text-decoration: none;
    border-color: var(--color-primary);
    transform: translateY(-2px);
  }

  ## 2. Create the currency detail page

  Add currency.html with these areas:

  - Back-to-market link
  - Currency name and symbol
  - Current exchange rate
  - Potential market influences
  - Related-news list
  - Loading, empty, and error states
  - Source and financial-information disclaimer

  Load dedicated files from it:

  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/currency.css" />
  <script src="/js/currency.js" type="module"></script>

  ## 3. Read and validate the selected currency

  In js/currency.js, read the URL parameters:

  const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
  const type = params.get('type');
  const symbol = params.get('symbol')?.toUpperCase();

  const allowedForex = ['EUR', 'GBP', 'JPY', 'CAD', 'AUD', 'CHF'];
  const allowedCrypto = ['BTC', 'ETH', 'BNB', 'XRP', 'SOL'];

  const allowedSymbols = type === 'forex' ? allowedForex : allowedCrypto;

  if (!['forex', 'crypto'].includes(type) || !allowedSymbols.includes(symbol)) {
    window.location.href = '/';
  }

  Never place arbitrary URL parameter values directly into innerHTML.

